body:
`kalide config` manages kalide's user-level settings — a flat key-to-string-value map persisted in a per-user config file under the operating system's user config directory (macOS `~/Library/Application Support/kalide/`, Linux `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/kalide/` or `~/.config/kalide/`, Windows `%AppData%\kalide\`), modelled on `git config`. The file is shared by every deck and every working directory and is created (with its directory) on the first write. `get`, `set` and `unset` accept recognised keys only: the recognised key set is `default-template-dir` (default-template-dir), and a key outside it is a usage error — usage to stderr, exit 2, listing the recognised keys, and nothing written. It has four subcommands:
- `kalide config get <key>` prints the value and exits 0 when the recognised key is set; a recognised key that is unset prints an error naming the key to stderr and exits non-zero.
- `kalide config set <key> <value>` writes the value into the user config file and exits 0.
- `kalide config unset <key>` removes the recognised key when present; unsetting a recognised-but-absent key is a no-op success.
- `kalide config list` prints one `key=value` line per set recognised key (no output when none are set) and exits 0.
A missing or unknown subcommand, a missing `<key>`, or a `set` missing its `<value>` is a usage error (usage to stderr, exit 2, nothing written). The store's sole recognised key is `default-template-dir` (default-template-dir). The command is local-filesystem only: it never reaches the network.

AC:
- `kalide config set default-template-dir ~/libs/deck-templates` writes an absolute path into the per-user config file, and `kalide config get default-template-dir` prints that absolute path and exits 0;
- `kalide config get default-template-dir` on a fresh config exits non-zero with a message naming the key; `kalide config set other-key x`, `kalide config get other-key` and `kalide config unset other-key` each exit 2 with a usage error listing the recognised keys (`default-template-dir`) and write nothing;
- `kalide config list` prints `default-template-dir=<absolute path>` after the set, and prints nothing on a fresh config;
- `kalide config unset default-template-dir` removes it and a following `get` exits non-zero; unsetting an absent recognised key exits 0;
- `kalide config` with no subcommand, or `set` with no value, is a usage error and writes nothing.
